Keep the timestamp test running past the hold-queue release.

Extends 107, and applies on top of it so the two windows can be measured
separately. Replaces an earlier version of this patch that used a "have we
delivered a picture yet" flag; that version was wrong, and the measurements
below are why.

107 filters the hold queue at release time. Four recordings per profile,
offset between the first audio packet and the first picture:

    profile      before 107       with 107 (median / worst)
    software        534 ms            130 / 226 ms
    vaapi          1391 ms            356 / 1052 ms
    qsv            1368 ms             85 / 110 ms

The VAAPI worst case is essentially the pre-patch figure, because gh_passthru
is armed as soon as the hold queue drains and every packet after that is
forwarded unfiltered.

The flag-based attempt removed that worst case (VAAPI worst 1052 -> 385 ms) but
left software untouched, and reading the block order in the recordings shows
why. Arrival order is not presentation order. The audio encoder lags the video
one on the delivery clock while still stamping its output with the original
stream times, so audio arrives *after* the first picture carrying a pts *before*
it. One software run, in file write order:

    VIDEO  pts  572 ms     <- delivered first
    audio  pts  277 ms
    audio  pts  298 ms
    audio  pts  320 ms       ... six of them, all stamped ahead of the picture
    VIDEO  pts  672 ms

A flag is set by that first picture and lets every one of those through, and the
player still has a third of a second of sound before it has anything to show.
The test has to stay on the timestamps, which is what 107 does and what this
patch now carries into passthru: the pts of the first picture is remembered at
release time, and any later audio stamped before it is dropped whatever moment
it arrives. Audio resumes on its own as soon as its pts passes that mark.

gh_first_video_pts is seeded to PTS_UNSET at creation -- calloc would leave it
at 0, a legitimate timestamp -- and reset in gh_flush(), which is the one place
every stop and restart path goes through.

Final measurement, five runs per encoder, offset between the first audio packet
and the first picture (negative = picture first, which is what a viewer wants):

    step                  SOFTWARE        VAAPI          QSV
                          med / worst     med / worst    med / worst
    none                   534 / 534      1391 / 1391    1368 / 1368
    107 alone              130 / 226       356 / 1052       85 / 110
    107 + 108 (flag)       280 / 295       304 /  385       53 /  93
    107 + 108 (pts)        -11 /   -5       -9 /    0      -18 /   -2

Fifteen measurements in the final row, every one at or below zero. See
106-108-av-start-HOWTO.txt for how the three patches divide the problem.

Not introduced by PR #2117: globalheaders.c is untouched by it.

--- src/plumbing/globalheaders.c.orig
+++ src/plumbing/globalheaders.c
@@ -27,6 +27,7 @@
   struct th_pktref_queue gh_holdq;
   streaming_start_t *gh_ss;
   int gh_passthru;
+  int64_t gh_first_video_pts;  /* pts of the first picture sent downstream */
 } globalheaders_t;
 
 // note: there up to 2.5 sec diffs in some sources!
@@ -70,6 +71,7 @@
   }
 
   pktref_clear_queue(&gh->gh_holdq);
+  gh->gh_first_video_pts = PTS_UNSET;
 }
 
 
@@ -465,6 +467,8 @@
           break;
         }
       }
+      /* carried into passthru so the same test keeps applying */
+      gh->gh_first_video_pts = first_video_pts;
 
       // Send all pending packets
       while((pkt = pktref_get_first(&gh->gh_holdq)) != NULL) {
@@ -553,6 +557,29 @@
     break;
   case SMT_PACKET:
     pkt = sm->sm_data;
+    /* Extends the hold-queue filter past the release, on the same test: a
+       packet is dropped when its pts precedes the first picture, whatever the
+       moment it arrives.
+
+       Arrival order is not presentation order here. The audio encoder lags the
+       video one on the delivery clock while still stamping its output with the
+       original stream times, so audio reaches this point *after* the first
+       picture yet carries a pts *before* it -- and would be presented ahead of
+       any image. Measured on one software run: the first picture was written
+       first at pts 572 ms, immediately followed by audio stamped 277 to 384 ms.
+       A "have we seen a picture yet" flag lets exactly that case through, which
+       is why the comparison has to stay on the timestamps. */
+    if (gh->gh_first_video_pts != PTS_UNSET && pkt->pkt_payload &&
+        pkt->pkt_pts != PTS_UNSET && pkt->pkt_pts < gh->gh_first_video_pts) {
+      streaming_start_component_t *vssc =
+        streaming_start_component_find_by_index(gh->gh_ss,
+                                                pkt->pkt_componentindex);
+      if (vssc && !SCT_ISVIDEO(vssc->es_type)) {
+        pkt_trace(LS_GLOBALHEADERS, pkt, "drop, precedes first picture");
+        streaming_msg_free(sm);
+        break;
+      }
+    }
     if (pkt->pkt_payload || pkt->pkt_err)
       streaming_target_deliver2(gh->gh_output, sm);
     else
@@ -601,6 +628,8 @@
 
   TAILQ_INIT(&gh->gh_holdq);
 
+  /* calloc leaves this at 0, which is a valid timestamp */
+  gh->gh_first_video_pts = PTS_UNSET;
   gh->gh_output = output;
   streaming_target_init(&gh->gh_input, &globalheaders_input_ops, gh, 0);
   return &gh->gh_input;
